                                   
  SHELL FILE AND FOLDER LINK SUPPORT FAR PLUGIN: DEVELOPMENT HISTORY

                  Version 1.0 (New Year beta) 3.01.98

     It  has  been  tested  in  two  months  under  Windows  95  OSR2  RUS  and
Windows  NT  workstation 4.0 RUS Service Pack 3. The supposition was  confirmed
that  OLE library interface functions work incorrect under Windows 95 OSR2  RUS
when  called  from  a  dynamic linked library. Because of  this  OLE  bug,  the
operating  system version checking procedure is included in shell link  support
module  functioning  algorithm.  If plugin  runs  under  Windows  95,  it  just
interacts  with  FAR  and  leaves  shell file  and  folder  link  creation  and
resolution  to  additional executable modules create.shl and  resolve.shl.  Per
anus ad astra.

                Version 1.1 (February release) 22.02.98

     Russian  named  folder  shell  links  haven't  been  resolved  because  of
misconversion from one Russian code page to another. It was fixed.
     Creating  group  of shell links, path to every next object  was  added  by
paths to previous one. It was fixed.

                Version 1.11 (You ask for it) 29.04.98

     An extra space has been added in "Description" field if description prefix
was empty. Anton Steblev (as@itti.com) found this bug. It was fixed.
     Now it's possible to set folder link mask to a different one from standard
*.lnk,  and  to  change  link  file  name case  when  creating.  Taras  Terexob
(2:5020/1333.10@fidonet, tepexob@i-connect.ru) asked for it.
     Creating  shell  link to an executable or batch file, "Working  Directory"
field  was empty. Alexey Petrov (alesha@chat.ru) asked this field to be set  to
linked objects' current directory.

                 Version 1.12 (Little bugfix) 8.05.98

     Creating  shell  link  to  an  object under Windows  NT,  fields  "Working
Directory"  and  "Command" have been swapped out. It was not  a  good  idea  to
release new version before May Celebrations. The bug was fixed.
     Little  misspells were also found in English manual. They  were  fixed  (I
hope:).

                  Version 1.14 (Cat service) 23.11.98

     The  bug  was  found  because of which shell link to  a  catalog  at  root
directory has not been recognized by MS Explorer. Actually, all the links  have
been  created incorrectly, although it was not acceptable using standard  shell
link interface functions. As usual, MS goes its own undocumented way. This  bug
was fixed.
     Shell  links  to objects with Russian (and, probably, any national)  names
have  been  created  in wrong code page under Windows 98. Certainly,  it  works
properly under NT: The bug was fixed.
     Russian named folder shell links haven't been resolved even under NT. Load
function  of  IPersistFile interface simply doesn't work. The same  code  works
normally when called from executable. It was fixed - i.e. additional executable
module resolves shell links to folder objects now.

               Version 1.15 (Tempora mutantur) 05.09.99

     They  say if nothing can help you, you should read the manual. Working  in
FAR  context,  I must call SetFileApisToAnsi() before any file operations  with
win32api  functions.  Thus,  we  avoid all  the  problems  with  Russian  names
described  above.  Helper  modules are moved off, therefore  plugin  will  work
faster.
     Shell links to objects selected into the temporary FAR panel haven't  been
created.  It  was  legal - it's impossible to create a link  to  emulated  file
system  object, to a file in archive for example. But it was not right  because
some  FAR plugins (such as temporary panel or network browser) give full access
to real files and folders. The bug was fixed.
     Creating  group  of shell links to some objects at current  folder,  those
links have been placed in a root directory. It was fixed.
     Creating shell link at alternative location, such as on desktop instead of
other panel current folder by pressing "On desktop" key (and vice versa),  that
link has been named by default regardless of user input. The bug was fixed.
     
     
